  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1233 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nNo significant changes are expected for the long term forecast. \nOn Friday, upper level southwesterly flow will be over the area \nahead of an upper level trough moving east through the western \nstates. At the surface, breezy southerly winds will advect Gulf \nmoisture and warm temperatures into west-central Texas. Look for \nhighs mainly in the upper 60s to lower 70s. By Saturday evening, \nthe western upper level trough will be moving east through the \ncentral and southern plains. A dryline will mix east during the \nlate afternoon hours, followed by a cold front bringing northerly \nwinds and cooler temperatures for Sunday through most of next \nweek. Before the cold front moves through however, we could have \nsome showers and thunderstorms develop along these surface \nboundaries with ample moisture available as well as upper level \nsupport from the through moving through. However, confidence is \nstill rather low in these rain chances (20%-30%). Dry north to \nnortheast surface winds will persist through Monday, allowing for \nhighs only in the 50s. Models have generally backed off on the \nstrength of the next trough, and precipitation potential from \nMonday night into Tuesday, but there are still low (20%) chances \nfor showers. Continued cool conditions expected into Wednesday.",
